#: ../../Residuals/Residuals_Generation_and_Management.rst:42
msgid "River water turbidity is often dominated by clay and silt particles, which are larger on average than kaolin clay. The larger particles have a smaller surface area per unit mass, which increases the mass per turbidity. The range of 0.7–2.0 mg/L per NTU is based on the observed range of turbidity per unit mass for different river waters. We will use :math:`k_T^C=0.001` kg/(m³·NTU) for preliminary calculations as this value should be within a factor of 2 and well within the range of uncertainty for residual management."
msgstr ""

